-The year 2019 witnessed a surge in the application and refinement of pre-trained language models (PLMs) across      
diverse NLP tasks. The BERT architecture continued to dominate, with works like Pretrained Encyclopedia demonstrating
that weakly supervised pretraining objectives explicitly incorporating real-world knowledge significantly outperform 
standard BERT on entity-related QA tasks [^10.48550_arxiv.1912.09637]. Similarly, AraNet leveraged bidirectional     
transformer encoders (BERT) to predict dialect, emotion, and sentiment in Arabic social media, delivering            
state-of-the-art results without manual feature engineering [^10.48550_arxiv.1912.13072]. The versatility of PLMs was
further proven in clinical settings, where Clinical XLNet modeled sequential clinical notes to predict prolonged     
mechanical ventilation, outperforming baselines by leveraging temporal information [^10.48550_arxiv.1912.11975]. In  
the realm of summarization, leveraging lead bias for zero-shot abstractive news summarization showed that            
self-supervised pretraining on large unlabeled corpora using BART and T5 dramatically improved generation quality    
[^10.48550_arxiv.1912.11602]. Furthermore, history aggregation mechanisms based on Transformers were proposed to     
tackle long-text dependencies in abstractive summarization [^10.48550_arxiv.1912.11046]. Multilingual and            
language-independent models also showed promise, proving comparable to monolingual approaches and highly effective   
for low-resource languages [^10.48550_arxiv.1912.13106]. End-to-end joint models for NER and relation extraction     
demonstrated that integrating large pre-trained language models without external NLP tools matches or exceeds        
state-of-the-art performance [^10.48550_arxiv.1912.13415].                                                           
